<p>&quot;The net outflow estimated at a whopping 85,000 million rupees in the month of September will create a severe strain on the liquidity in the banking system. Yields at the short-end could be seen reacting by 50-100 basis points and volumes are likely to be low.</p>
<p>The refinance facility at 11 percent against export credit which stands at about 120,000 million rupees will be substantially drawn down. An improvement in the credit off-take which has so far been negative, with a net repayment of advances since April, could compound the crisis.</p>
<p>However, a closer look at the cash flows suggests that most of the drain is due to occur in the second fortnight of the month and the first half might still be fairly easy.</p>
<p>A good strategy would be to keep away from anything with more than two year residual maturity and stay invested in near cash assets such as call money or repos for the next fortnight.</p>
<p>A drop in prices towards late September would offer opportunities to buy good short maturity assets at higher yields. Impact on yields of longer duration assets however, is expected to be minimal though volumes will dry down to a trickle.&quot;</p>
<p>Source-I-Sec debt market update</p>
